  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Urban Resilience, Inclusion and Socio-Economic Empowerment (Simuka),</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Simuka Programme is a multiyear, SDC  funded urban resilience initiative implemented by a GOAL  led consortium (GOAL, CAFOD, Plan International, CCJP and Silveira House) in Bulawayo, Gweru  and Kwekwe. The programme aims to strengthen economic resilience, inclusive urban governance, and socio economic empowerment of vulnerable urban populations&#x2014;particularly women, youth, persons with disabilities, and informal economic actors.
Zimbabwe&#x2019;s urban context presents complex political economy constraints, including:
&#x2022;	Centralized control over local government decision  making;
&#x2022;	Politicization of regulatory and service delivery systems;
&#x2022;	Rapid growth of informal economic activities;
&#x2022;	High poverty levels and shrinking economic opportunities
&#x2022;	Increasing climate shocks and stressors
&#x2022;	Gendered and exclusionary power relations affecting market access and voice.
In line with the consortium&#x2019;s commitment to context sensitive , adaptive and politically informed programming, Simuka applies:
&#x2022;	Thinking and Working Politically (TWP);
&#x2022;	Actor Based  Change (ABC);
&#x2022;	Voice and Accountability (V&amp;A);
&#x2022;	A systems approach through the Resilient and Inclusive Neighbourhood Approach (RINA).</narrative>
  </description>
